---
// A plain-language explanation of Bayes factors, the Cauchy prior and effect sizes, shown above the Bayes charts.
import { FUNDER_OZER } from '../utils/effects';
---
<div class="bg">
  <p>
    A p value can only say whether a difference was detected. When it was not, it cannot tell you whether the difference is truly absent or whether the study was too small to see it.
    A <strong>Bayes factor</strong> can. It asks how many times more likely the data are under one story than under the other: there is no difference, or there is a difference of a plausible size.
    If the data are 10 times more likely when there is no difference, that is a Bayes factor of 10 for no difference.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second story needs a guess about "a plausible size," so I ran it three ways: a <strong>small</strong>, a <strong>medium</strong> and a <strong>large</strong> effect, using the benchmarks
    Funder and Ozer (2019) proposed for psychology. The guess is a <strong>Cauchy prior</strong>, a bell-shaped curve centered on zero with long tails, so smaller effects are most likely and larger ones are not ruled out.
    Each row of a chart shows all three. When the three markers sit close together, the conclusion does not depend on the guess. When they spread out, it does.
  </p>
  <table class="scale">
    <thead><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Correlation (r)</th><th>Cohen's d</th><th>What it means in daily life</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Very small</td><td>.05</td><td>0.10</td><td>Hard to see in one person, but it can add up over many people or many years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Small</td><td>.10</td><td>0.20</td><td>Noticeable only when you look across many cases</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Medium</td><td>.20</td><td>0.41</td><td>A typical finding in published psychology, and a real difference in everyday life</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Large</td><td>.30</td><td>0.63</td><td>Rare in psychology, and clearly visible</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Very large</td><td>.40 or more</td><td>0.87 or more</td><td>Uncommon, and often an overestimate</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="small">
    These benchmarks are more realistic than the older 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8 rule of thumb, which describes effects most psychology studies never reach. This site labels every effect size with them.
    For ratings from the same person, d is the mean difference divided by the spread of the differences, and I convert it to r the way you would for two equal groups, so the labels are a guide.
  </p>
  <table class="scale two">
    <thead><tr><th>Times more likely</th><th>How to describe the evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 to 3</td><td>Anecdotal: barely worth mentioning</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 to 10</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 to 30</td><td>Strong</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30 to 100</td><td>Very strong</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 100</td><td>Extreme</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="small">
    The same words apply in both directions. Data that are 12 times more likely under no difference are strong evidence for no difference, and data 12 times more likely under a difference are strong evidence for a difference.
    Near 1, the data cannot tell the two stories apart.
  </p>
</div>
<style>
  .bg { max-width: 66ch; font-family: var(--display); }
  .bg p { margin: 0.7rem 0; color: var(--ink); }
  .bg .small { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .scale { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0.8rem 0; font-size: 0.93rem; width: 100%; }
  .scale th, .scale td { text-align: left; padding: 0.3rem 1rem 0.3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; }
  .scale th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; }
  .scale.two { width: auto; }
</style>
